course: dictionary
lesson: 8517
title: Saturate: soaked through, or full up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Saturate has a few meanings, but they all share one idea: something is filled so completely that no more can fit or soak in. Use the words around it to find which meaning fits. «Water can hold only a certain amount of salt; after that it is saturated and extra salt settles at the bottom.» Here, saturated means the water cannot take any more salt.
explain: Rain can saturate your clothes, so they are soaked right through. Saturated ground is so wet that rain cannot soak in, which is why fields can flood. The clue is what is being filled: water, clothes, or ground.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If "soaked through" or "full up" still makes sense, you are on the right track. You will often meet this word in science and geography lessons.
fact: Saturate is a verb: saturate, saturates, saturated, saturating.
fact: «Saturate» means to make something completely wet, soaked through.
fact: In science, a «saturated solution» is a liquid that holds as much of a substance as it can, so no more will dissolve.
fact: «Saturated ground» is so wet that rain cannot soak in, which can cause flooding.
fact: Phrases to know: «saturated with», «saturated solution», «saturated fat».
exercise: «Water can hold only a certain amount of salt; after that it is saturated.» In this sentence, saturated means | The water is very cold | The water has turned into ice | The salt is sweet | *The liquid cannot hold any more salt
exercise: After the storm, the ground was saturated and the fields flooded. Why did the fields flood? | The farmer opened the gates | *Rain could not soak into the ground | The fields were covered in snow | The ground was too dry to hold water
exercise: «The bag was saturated with juice.» What does saturated with mean here? | Completely empty of juice | Shaped like a juice carton | Cold from the fridge | *Soaked through with juice
test: «The sponge was saturated after the spill.» What does saturated mean here? | Very light | *Soaked through, with no room for more water | Dried out in the sun | Shaped like a sponge
test: In a saturated solution, what happens to extra salt? | *It settles at the bottom | It disappears completely | It floats on top like oil | It turns into sugar
exam: Which sentence uses saturate to mean filled so full that nothing more can fit? | The market was soaked in the rain and closed early | *The market was saturated with products, so no more stalls could fit | The market was painted bright blue | The market was quiet and empty
